Beryllium: named utility services can be locked down as renderers are, and every process marks where its sandbox would begin

Purpose: plan/18 section 4.2, tier 1 (feature matrix S-PRC-01, S-PRC-08;
  decided 12 November 2026: a policy for the utilities that only compute,
  next after the renderer). Some utility services take bytes over Mojo and
  give bytes back. They can use the renderer's lockdown unchanged; the work
  is knowing which they are, by service name, since sandbox type is too
  coarse (the storage service has the same type and holds the profile's
  databases open for writing).
  The browser decides, the utility is told, and the kernel is asked:
  - base/posix/capsicum_rights.{h,cc}: the feature
    BerylliumCapsicumUtilities, off by default;
    BrowserConfinesUtility(service), true with the feature on for three
    candidates (data_decoder.mojom.DataDecoderService,
    unzip.mojom.Unzipper, patch.mojom.FilePatcher) and for any service
    named in the browser's test switch --beryllium-confine-services=a,b,
    which is how a service is tried before it joins the list.
  - content/browser/child_process_launcher_helper.{h,cc}: for such a
    utility the launcher appends --beryllium-lockdown to its command line,
    registers it as a confined peer (so what it is sent is cut, as for a
    renderer) and holds its invitation until the kernel says capability
    mode (beryllium-invite-after-lockdown.patch; for a utility that order
    cannot be switched off, since it has no other check). A utility that
    does not lock down is ended without having been sent anything. The
    browser records it as attested when it invites it.
  - sandbox/policy/freebsd/sandbox_freebsd.cc: a utility that finds
    --beryllium-lockdown on its command line locks down in
    InitializeSandbox(), which utility_main calls before any thread is
    made: the same warm-up, descriptor sweep, hardening calls and
    cap_enter() as a renderer, and the same end if any of it fails. Its
    log lines say "utility pid=N service=NAME" where a renderer's say
    "renderer pid=N", and the trap report carries the same word.
    Every other process (the browser, the GPU process, the services that
    are not confined) now writes the survey's marker at the same point, so
    the Capsicum survey can say for each kind of process what capability
    mode would refuse it after its start. That list is what decides
    whether a service can go on the list above; until 0.20.3.21 only
    renderers wrote the marker.
  - chrome/browser/ui/webui/sandbox/sandbox_internals_ui.cc: a confined
    utility's line on beryllium://sandbox says lockdown requested: yes and
    whether it has been attested.
  The three candidates are chosen by what they are for, not measured: no
  survey has seen a short-lived utility after its start. With the feature
  on, one of them may end at its lockdown (a descriptor outside the
  policy) or be refused a call it needs; the lockdown's log and the trap
  report say which. That is what the feature being off by default means.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on
  beryllium-invite-after-lockdown.patch, beryllium-kernel-check.patch,
  beryllium-capability-trap-report.patch and
  beryllium-sandbox-status-page.patch.
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. Not yet
  compiled (written for build 0.20.3.22). base/posix/capsicum_rights.cc
  and the lockdown's code with InitializeSandbox() type-check against
  FreeBSD 16's headers with stand-ins for Chromium's declarations.
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/tests/test_50_sandbox.py, class
  ConfinedUtilities: a browser with the lockdown, the feature and the trap
  report; every utility that says it locked down must be in capability
  mode by the kernel and invited only after that, and what each was
  refused is listed. test_capability_mode_per_process requires every
  process to be confined or not as its kind is expected to be.
security-review: required (sandbox setup, process launch, IPC boundary)

