+// Beryllium (plan/18 section 4.2, tier 1; feature matrix S-PRC-01): utility
+// services that take bytes over Mojo and give bytes back can be confined as
+// a renderer is. Which services those are is decided in the browser, which
+// then tells the utility to lock down, registers it as a confined peer (so
+// what it is sent is cut) and sends it nothing until the kernel says it is
+// in capability mode. Off by default: the list below has not been measured
+// on a machine. --enable-features=BerylliumCapsicumUtilities switches it on.
+BASE_EXPORT BASE_DECLARE_FEATURE(kBerylliumCapsicumUtilities);
+
+// Given by the browser to a utility process it has decided to confine; the
+// utility then locks down in InitializeSandbox(). Read from a child's own
+// command line only, which the browser wrote.
+BASE_EXPORT extern const char kBerylliumLockdownSwitch[];
+
+// A test switch of the browser: further services to confine, by the names
+// --utility-sub-type carries, separated by commas. It is how a service is
+// tried before it is put on the list. Read only when the feature is on.
+BASE_EXPORT extern const char kBerylliumConfineServicesSwitch[];
+
+// In the browser: whether the utility service |sub_type| is to be confined.
+BASE_EXPORT bool BrowserConfinesUtility(std::string_view sub_type);
